WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.670
LUCAS: Good evening.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.990
My name is Lucas, and I'm
a senior premed student

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.230
from Phoenix, Arizona,
and it is my pleasure

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.170
to introduce your
speaker for tonight.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.250
Victor Davis Hanson, the Wayne
and Marcia Buske Distinguished

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.710
Fellow in History at
Hillsdale College,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.250
is also a senior fellow
at the Hoover Institution

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:34.370
and a professor of classics
emeritus at California State

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.250
University, Fresno.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.920
He earned his BA
at the University

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.710
of California, Santa
Cruz, and his PhD

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.470
in classics from
Stanford University.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.670
In 2007, he was awarded the
National Humanities Medal,

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.970
and in 2008, he received
the Bradley Prize

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.390
from Lind and Harry
Bradley Foundation.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.130
He has written for
numerous publications,

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.320
including the Claremont Review
of Books, The New Criterion,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.239
and The Wall Street Journal.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.080
He is the author
of several books,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.480
including A War Like No Other,
How the Athenians and Spartans

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.700
Fought the Peloponnesian
War, and The Second World

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.900
Wars, How the First Global
Conflict was Fought and Won.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.630
Would you all please welcome
me in joining Victor Davis

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.710
Hanson to the stage?

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.119
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:32.963 --> 00:01:36.372
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Thank you very much.

00:01:36.372 --> 00:01:40.300
AUDIENCE: Give it
to us straight.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.090
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: When
Doctor Arnn mentioned stitches

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.250
in the face, I get a lot of
emails from people who say,

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.623
I think you need some
work on your face.

00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:51.790
I said, I've already had it.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.700
But Al Phillip and I were
supposed to go on a trip

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.550
seven days later, so I had a
concussion, a spinal injury,

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.110
and I had 170 stitches.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.010
And I asked Al how
much money we would

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:10.330
lose with all the cancellations,
so suddenly, I got cured.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:18.170
And I wanted to tell
Larry, when he mentioned

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.720
all of these
disasters, I thought,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.037
I hope Hillsdale's not
the common denominator.

00:02:25.037 --> 00:02:26.120
But it hasn't been for me.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.750
It's been a wonderful experience
for 15 years coming here.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.320
I'd like to talk just
about 35 minutes,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.870
not just about George
Patton and the tragic,

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:39.470
view but our strange and
ambiguous relationship

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:44.210
in the United States between
generals and politics.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:48.470
We've had 12 presidents that
were generals, but none of us

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.150
really know any of them.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:55.800
And who cares whether Chester
A. Arthur was a general?

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.430
We remember Harrison
and his grandson,

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:04.340
or Pierce, or Taylor, but
most of the generals--

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:05.210
Andrew Johnson?

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.670
Who cares that he was
a brigadier general?

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.640
We have four that
became presidents.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.140
With the exception
of Jackson, they

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.780
were pretty much apolitical.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.300
By that, I mean Grant was
a Unionist more than he

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.580
was a radical Republican.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.230
And I think both Democrats
and Republicans approached

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:29.400
Eisenhower, and no one was
quite sure what side he was on.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.775
And Washington, of course,
was the father of our country.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.990
By the way, we had three
generals, remember, working

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.390
for Donald Trump at one time--

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.640
General Kelly, the
chief of staff,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:48.493
General Mattis, Secretary
of Defense, and HR McMaster,

00:03:48.493 --> 00:03:50.660
lieutenant general, was a
national security advisor.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.610
I have a feeling two
out of the three of them

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:55.640
either didn't vote for Trump or
won't vote for him next time.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.130
So I guess they're called
bipartisan or apolitical,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.744
but we have had
ideological generals.

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.550
And I don't know if ideology
is the right word for it,

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.220
but we've had
generals who connected

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.070
what they did on the
battlefield with a larger

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.500
view of human nature.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.040
And I guess we would call it
the tragic, or pessimistic, view

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.540
that we are born into this
world pretty despicable or evil,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.650
and religion, and culture,
and civilization save us.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.870
But it doesn't always
reach everybody,

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.760
and there's always
going to be people

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.620
who want to take things
that are not theirs,

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.080
whether on the personal
or national level,

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.120
or they're psychopathic and
they like to kill people.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.090
And it's the duty
of certain people

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:43.450
among us-- this view, I
think some generals have--

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:47.980
that they have certain talents,
and they spot these people,

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.440
and they're willing to do
what it takes to protect

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.660
the innocent from them.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.020
And out of that
ideology, they also

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:00.100
develop a conservative or
reactionary political view.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.450
And you think of MacArthur,
but in our own history,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:07.930
think of William Tecumseh
Sherman, Curtis LeMay,

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.090
and Matthew Ridgway.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.920
And they don't end up well.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.570
They have a dark
view of human nature,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.940
and we're a therapeutic, sunny
society, an upbeat society.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.490
And when we're done with
them, we're done with them.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.070
They provide a useful service.

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.920
Sherman said, I will
not run for office.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.230
If nominated, I will not
accept the nomination.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:29.440
If selected, I will not
enter the presidency.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.280
And people thought, to this
day, that he was a barn-burner,

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:33.868
that he was--

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:35.410
I see the word
terrorist and Sherman.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.770
Sherman had a philosophical
view, a tragic view,

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.400
that they were in an existential
war and a lot of innocent

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.850
people were getting killed, and
the people who had precipitated

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:51.250
that war-- he called them the
Chevalier class in the South--

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.320
should not function
with impunity at home.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.470
And so when he went to
Atlanta and the March

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.530
to the Sea and the
March to the Carolinas,

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:01.390
if you actually do the terrible
arithmetic of how many people

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:05.350
he killed vis-a-vis that summer
of the Army of the Potomac

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.290
with Grant locked with Lee,
there were far fewer dead,

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:10.660
but he did something
that was unspeakable.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:14.050
He destroyed property, and he
humiliated an entire class,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.990
and he developed an
ideology and explication

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.110
of what he was doing and
why they deserved it.

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.540
And that was intolerable.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:27.460
And to this day, Grant
had been responsible

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:28.960
for some of the
most bloody battles

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.550
in the history of American
wars, but Sherman has

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.650
an asterisk next to his name.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.460
The same is true
of another general.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:39.850
We all love Hap Arnold.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.820
We hear of Tooey
Spatz, Doolittle,

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:46.240
but when we say
Curtis LeMay, we stop.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.860
Some of you think the cigar--

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.340
we don't ever hear that he
had Bell's palsy on a record

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:52.210
flight.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:55.480
Set the distance
record, 1937, in a B-17.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.300
On the way in the flight--
he got Bell's palsy--

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:03.050
stuck a cigar there to absorb
the dripping of saliva.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.800
So it wasn't some Hollywood
gimmick to be tough,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.480
it was a way of trying to
disguise a disfiguring ailment.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.962
But he ended up after
the war, remember,

00:07:13.962 --> 00:07:17.710
as the model of Buck
Turgidson in Dr. Strangelove.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:19.720
And then during the
Cuban Missile Crisis,

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:21.400
people caricatured
him because he

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.740
wanted to be pretty tough
on the Soviet Union.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.490
And he ended up
running, tragically,

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:29.860
with George Wallace
as his vice president

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:33.790
candidate, third-party American
Independent Party, 1968.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:37.780
We forget that he actually
tried to convince Wallace not

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.970
to run on a segregationist
or racist ticket,

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:41.980
and he was one of the
first green candidates

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.480
we've ever had.

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:47.440
But we had this
image that LeMay said

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.320
that it would be inhuman
for a million people

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.602
to die invading the
mainland of Japan

00:07:53.602 --> 00:07:55.060
and he could do it
through bombing.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:58.890
And he inherited the B-29
billion-dollar program--

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.580
$2 billion, bigger
than the Manhattan

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:04.240
Project-- and they had no
results from China, India,

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.400
or from the Marianas.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.310
And he took this wonderful,
high-precision, high-altitude

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:12.940
bomber that could fly
up to 28,000 feet,

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:14.800
and he took it
down to 5,000 feet

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.560
and he basically created
a four-engine dive bomber

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.940
and filled it with
20,000 pounds of napalm

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.690
and in 90 days, burned down 65%
of the urban industrial area

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.400
of Japan.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:32.980
And he was adamantly opposed
to the 509th Composite Bombing

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:37.039
Group because he didn't think
the atomic bomb was necessary.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.760
In other words, he thought
that he could, as he said,

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:44.410
render Japan into
a pre-modern state.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:47.230
Had the war gone
on, he had plans

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:51.170
to use four airfields from
Okinawa and to bring--

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.890
remember, the war in
Europe had ended in May--

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:58.880
and there were 10,000
four-engine B-24, and B-17,

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.850
and Lancaster bombers
that were idle,

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:03.980
another 8,000
two-engine bombers--

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.350
B-26s and B-25.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.110
He was going to
aggregate them with 2,500

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:12.860
B-29s in the Marianas,
triple the mission capacity

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.140
because Okinawa was a
third of the distance,

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:19.250
and finish off the rest of the
Japanese industrial sector.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.470
It's pretty
nightmarish scenario.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.740
But the idea that he
was behind all that--

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:26.600
remember, he said,
if we lose the war,

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:29.030
I'll be tried as
a war criminal--

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.700
was that we're not
going to send people

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.000
who had nothing to do with this
war from Nebraska and Missouri

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.210
and invade the mainland when
people who were actively

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:41.090
killing 15 million
people in China

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:45.070
are going to escape a
rendezvous with judgment.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.940
That's what he said.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:52.430
Matthew Ridgway inherited,
in late December

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:56.210
of 1950, a losing Korean War.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:58.790
Remember, the Chinese
had crossed the Yalu.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.920
We had gone up in November--

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:07.100
MacArthur had, and
General Walker.

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:10.470
As you go north, the
peninsula widens.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:16.310
In November, the weather worsens
and the proximity to China

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:17.960
decreases.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.110
And the F-80 was not able
to provide air support

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:24.170
against a MiG-15 for B-29s.

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:28.280
So essentially, we sent 150,000
people farther and farther

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:32.810
from their supplies and closer
to a million Chinese soldiers,

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.960
and we didn't have adequate air
cover in the height of winter.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:37.860
And when they broke
across, it was

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.540
the longest military
retreat in US history,

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:45.110
and MacArthur was
relieved, and Ridgway,

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.970
who had never been to Asia--

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.140
he was an expert
in Central America,

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.670
and of course, he was
commander of the 82nd Airborne.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:55.730
He created, basically, the
82nd Airborne in World War II.

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:59.240
50 years old, he
parachuted into Normandy.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:02.330
Distinguished record
in World War II.

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.670
I suggest that if any of
you have heart trouble,

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:05.580
don't despair.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.130
He had a heart
attack, and was told

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:09.680
he had to be
decommissioned and go back

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.280
into civilian service at
54 at the end of the war,

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:15.740
and he lived to be 98.

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.110
So don't despair.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:23.030
But in any case, he wore
a little medical kit here

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.350
and a hand grenade here.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.750
They called him Old Iron Tits.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:31.190
And he walked the battlefield.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.920
He said, find them, fix
them, and kill them.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.320
And he said, don't despair,
we're going to take Seoul back.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.600
They took Seoul back by March.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:45.860
The B-29 napalm and
artillery killed,

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:47.600
as we heard today
quite correctly,

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:49.640
about a million Chinese.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.790
One reason that China did
not want to invade in Vietnam

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:54.620
is because of the
terrible losses

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:58.970
that they hid from
historians in the West

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:03.440
that were incurred by Ridgway
in that counter-offensive.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:08.660
And then Ridgway got, after
basically saving and taking

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.910
back Seoul, what was
the reward for it?

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.940
Eisenhower, when he
wrote his memoirs,

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:17.300
said that Matthew Ridgway
didn't take Seoul.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.670
Crusade in Europe-- it's there.

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.840
He hated him so much.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:27.820
And when Eisenhower
wanted to go in Vietnam,

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:28.730
he called in Ridgway.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:29.480
Said, don't do it.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.060
You can't win a
land war in Asia.

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:37.250
Johnson went into Vietnam and he
thought, it's time to get out.

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:38.390
I'll get that old Ridgway.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:39.780
He'll give me the same advice.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.060
And he said, I got to get out.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.702
And Ridgway says, can't get out.

00:12:43.702 --> 00:12:44.910
He said, why can't I get out?

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:48.540
He said, there's
only one thing worse

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:51.470
than getting into a bad
war, and that's losing it.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.620
So he gave people advice and
he talked in a certain manner.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:56.720
He was married
three times, which

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.450
was a no-no at that
time in the top echelons

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.640
of the American general.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.570
And he didn't end up well.

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:05.570
He didn't get the type
of credit that he should,

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:07.778
in the way that LeMay didn't
and the way that Sherman

00:13:07.778 --> 00:13:09.690
didn't, which all brings
us to George Patton.

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:18.050
I wish I could say that
he had superb character.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:20.780
He did, in many ways,
but I think in many ways

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.320
he was Trumpian, if I could.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.500
And that's not meant as
a criticism gratuitously.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:29.110
By that, I mean he could be
gratuitously cruel to people.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:35.150
He, as you know, slapped two
soldiers in Sicily, one of whom

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:36.630
probably had malaria.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:39.020
We know had a high temperature.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.890
He was resented by
most of his peers

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:46.440
because he was from one of the
wealthiest families in America.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:46.940
Mt.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:50.510
Wilson in LA-- that was his
grandmother, the Wilson family.

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:52.310
His father was the
city attorney of LA

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:55.220
and owned a thousand
acres in Pasadena.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:58.030
He was fabulously rich
on his own family's side,

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:01.270
and then he married into the
Ayer Pharmaceutical Company,

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:03.820
Frederick Ayer's big empire.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:07.690
So when the image
of American officers

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:10.480
was Omar Bradley, and
Eisenhower, and Lucian

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:14.230
Truscott, and Wade Haislip,
all of these great people

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:16.030
from the hinterland
of America, here

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:22.060
came Patton from
California playing polo

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.400
with his own yacht and
a stable of horses,

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:26.440
all during the Depression.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.620
And he had been in
the 1912 Olympics.

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:30.640
He came in fourth.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:32.050
He might have won
the pentathlon.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.690
He claimed that he was such
a good shot that each time he

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.127
shot, he put the bullet
right through the prior hole

00:14:37.127 --> 00:14:38.710
and the judges didn't
understand that.

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:40.290
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:43.180
And he may have been right.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:49.930
But if you follow his career
through the '20s and '30s,

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.480
up until Pearl Harbor,
It was characterized

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:54.340
by absolute brilliance.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:58.870
He was the first person to see
that the Christie tank in 1919

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:02.230
had the best suspension and
the Americans should go for it,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:05.200
and yet we didn't do it and
that was the model that the T-34

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.370
Russian tank adopted.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:10.570
He designed the US
Calvary's saber.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:15.670
He'd also designed the
first US tank suit.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:18.850
I think it was called the Green
Arrow or the Green Lantern.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:19.850
It was kind of silly.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:23.410
It had a gold helmet, and
green pads, and gaudy boots,

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:27.220
but it was built to be
comfortable-- a jumpsuit.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:31.070
And in this entire
process, he learned--

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.320
or I shouldn't say he learned.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:35.530
He developed US armor tactics.

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:43.180
1940 in war games in Louisiana,
he captured the Senior General

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:44.320
Hugh Drum.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.830
You may have seen The
Dirty Dozen, that old movie

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:49.510
about how they played dirty.

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:53.350
That was based on
Patton's war maneuvers,

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.990
about how he went on a
400-mile goose chase,

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:58.880
they thought, and ended up
capturing the red general.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:00.250
He was on the blue team.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:02.170
And he did that two times.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:06.160
Then he went down
and got into Indio,

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.870
out the middle of nowhere, and
set up an entire desert warfare

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:14.380
complex and taught Americans,
with inferior tanks,

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:21.430
light Chaffee tanks
or inferior Lee tanks,

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:25.210
the elements of armor
pursuit and breakthroughs.

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:29.210
The point I'm making
is that he was 55

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:31.870
and he was still not
a brigadier general.

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:36.550
People hated him because
he drank too much.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:40.030
There were periods in his
life when he womanized.

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:42.760
He played polo, as I said.

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:45.470
He was accident-prone.

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:48.700
He lit a gas lamp
to look at his eye

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:51.250
and it blew up and
burned his face.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.730
He accidentally stabbed himself.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:56.640
In a horse accident,
he broke his leg.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.530
He got phelebitis.

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.700
And of course, he died in
a freak accident, as well.

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:06.569
So he was known as injury-prone,
reckless, rich, ostentatious,

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:09.609
loud, and yet, he spoke
French, read German,

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:11.920
and was highly educated.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:16.089
The point I'm getting at is that
he enunciated or he articulated

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:17.890
a world view of war.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:21.460
And it was similar to
his contemporaries,

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:25.089
like LeMay and Ridgway,
and also very Shermanesque.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:28.089
He was a big admirer of
William Tecumseh Sherman.

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:31.810
And he basically
said that democracies

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:35.170
are therapeutic societies and we
don't train people, thank God,

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:36.370
to kill people.

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.040
But there are people
in the world who do.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:40.660
And when they do,
they need people

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:44.230
like George Patton, who's
part of, and yet not part of,

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.950
a democracy that
understands the evil mind

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:51.790
and can make soldiers,
for brief periods of time,

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.530
have the training,
and the courage,

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:59.020
and the fortitude to stand up
to the Hermann Goring division,

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:03.580
or Focke-Wulf 190
pilots, or U-boats.

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:05.260
And that was his principle.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:07.240
And then you would
kill these evil people

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:09.840
and you would protect the
innocent, and he said that,

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.070
and we don't like
people to say that.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.907
When Colin Powell said,
what's your strategy

00:18:14.907 --> 00:18:16.490
for the first Gulf
War, he said, we're

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:21.327
going to find Saddam's army,
we're going to cut it off,

00:18:21.327 --> 00:18:22.660
and then we're going to kill it.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:23.650
And people got very angry.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:25.400
Why did he have to say
kill it at the end?

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:26.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.920
Remember when we
killed Baghdadi,

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.130
Nancy Pelosi was mad
that Trump said that.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:35.068
Why did he kill everybody?

00:18:35.068 --> 00:18:36.610
And then when Trump
said, we're going

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.910
to get rid of these monster and
these animals, MS-13, she said,

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.860
they're all God's creatures.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:45.940
So that therapeutic
alternative is deeply ingrained

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:48.520
and it's very hard
for societies like us

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.760
to mobilize against
these perceived threats

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:54.680
without these types of people.

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:59.130
So when we were ready after
Pearl Harbor to fight,

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.710
the obvious choice for
our first engagement

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:02.780
was George S. Patton.

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:09.570
Right before Pearl
Harbor, in October,

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:13.170
he was promoted to major
general, two-star general.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:17.130
And yet, when we had Operation
Torch, the November 1942

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:21.390
landings in Northwest
Africa, he was not

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:25.530
chosen to lead the
entire projective torch.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:28.890
He was given just the Western
command, 30,000 troops.

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:34.270
The most incompetent, useless
general in American history,

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:36.200
Lloyd Fredendall, was.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:37.860
And Eisenhower wrote
a report and said,

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:39.000
he looks like a general.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:40.210
He breathes fire.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:40.980
He's our man.

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:42.480
I've never been more impressed.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:43.780
He would swagger around.

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:45.363
He would pound his fists.

00:19:45.363 --> 00:19:46.530
And he didn't know anything.

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.080
And the result was, as
you know, the worst defeat

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:52.230
in American history, really, or
at least the most humiliating,

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:57.810
the Kasserine Pass, where Rommel
destroyed an entire brigade.

00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:02.458
3,000 missing, 400 dead,
600 tanks just fighting.

00:20:02.458 --> 00:20:03.000
Where was he?

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:07.830
50 miles back dug in in
a bunker, probably drunk.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:10.590
When it was time to
take over Second Corps,

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.180
everybody thought, Patton
will get his chance.

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:16.440
And yet, Eisenhower
asked General Harmon

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:19.890
to do it, who turned down, said,
this is pretty embarrassing.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:21.510
Patton deserves it.

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:29.310
So Patton took over immediately
at the Battle of El Guettar.

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:31.650
He won the first
battle Americans

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.630
had won in World War II.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:37.110
And he wanted to continue
ahead of Second Corps.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:38.850
And then Omar Bradley--

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:42.210
he had a very strange
relationship with Bradley.

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:48.210
And the movie was
quite, I think,

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:51.090
misleading about the attitude
of Bradley toward Patton.

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.940
But Bradley then was given
command of Second Corps

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.360
and Patton was supposed
to plan Sicily.

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.090
You would thought that too many
people had died in North Africa

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:05.430
needlessly without giving
Patton the main responsibility

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:08.940
for the invasion of
Sicily, Operation Husky.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.910
And yet, we deferred
to General Montgomery.

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:14.880
Both the Seventh Army
and the British 1st Army

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:16.770
landed in southern Sicily.

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:18.755
Everybody knew what the aim was.

00:21:18.755 --> 00:21:23.040
It was to get to Messinia, two
miles from the Italian coast,

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:26.280
cut it off, and cut
off 400,000 troops.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.710
And Patton looked
at his assignment.

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.800
It was to stay to the left,
or west, of Montgomery,

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:35.730
and protect his flank, and
be stationary, basically,

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:40.200
and give Montgomery the chance
to speed right to Messinia

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:43.210
and then trap the entire Italian
and German-- and of course,

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:44.940
he knew Monty wouldn't do that.

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:46.830
Monty was a great
general and a set piece,

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:48.210
but he was not a pursuer.

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:49.530
He didn't pursue.

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:50.740
So what did Patton do?

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:53.560
He went all the way
northwest to Palermo,

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:55.710
then made another right
turn, broke orders,

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:58.860
and got to Messinia
before Montgomery.

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:01.590
And of course, didn't or
didn't get there in time

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:05.570
to trap the Germans, but he
became very famous after that.

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:07.780
And you'd think
that at that point,

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:11.520
everybody knew that Overlord
was being planned in conjunction

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.460
with the Italian invasion, that
he would get a supreme command.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:17.640
He slapped two soldiers
quite despicably.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:18.660
He went into a hospital.

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:22.410
He was mad because the absentee
rate of soldiers for what we

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:25.770
would call post-traumatic
stress syndrome,

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:27.660
what was known then
as shell shock--

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:35.140
there were three officers who
watched the first slapping

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:35.640
incident.

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:38.130
The person had malaria
when he was slapped.

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:41.640
Second one, two weeks
later, he had some ailment,

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:44.580
whether it was a fever or it
was just stress, we don't know,

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.410
but he slapped him.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:51.510
In this period, one of
his armored companies

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:52.500
was on a bridge.

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:55.560
There was an Italian
farmer with two mules.

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:57.120
They were being strafed.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.690
They didn't want to
run over the mules.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:01.080
It was a very narrow bridge.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:07.350
Patton went up, took out
his .357 in his right hand,

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:10.500
and he had a .45 Colt in the
other, and shot these two mules

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:12.140
and had them thrown
over the bridge.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:13.950
And this was
considered terrible.

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:16.260
Think of this
therapeutic mindset.

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:18.593
Here, you have a
whole column stopped,

00:23:18.593 --> 00:23:20.010
and the papers and
journalists are

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:23.250
angry that Patton shot two mules
and threw them over the bridge

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:26.340
to facilitate the
company getting out

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.180
of a strafing attack.

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:30.450
But that was a very
important point, though,

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:33.120
because he obviously
should have been given one

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:36.900
of three possible appointments.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:40.590
One would have been
overall commander in Italy,

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:46.470
and that went to Mark Clark,
who we know mostly from falling

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:51.240
for the German gambit,
where basically,

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:54.690
the German forces
under Kessling allowed

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:58.592
us to take Rome as a way
to escape an encirclement.

00:23:58.592 --> 00:24:00.550
And they knew that Mark
Clark, of all generals,

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:03.310
would want to go
into Rome, especially

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:05.740
during the D-day operations
and get some glory.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.320
And then that would
give them a chance

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:11.800
to escape the encirclement, and
they predicted it perfectly.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:13.750
Patton wouldn't have done that.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:18.110
On the Anzio,
earlier in January,

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:20.350
remember, that was a
tricky amphibious operation

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:24.430
to land at the main port or
near the main port of Rome.

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:26.260
People didn't know
whether we could do it.

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:26.890
Surprise.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:27.790
We had a beachhead.

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:31.450
There was only one
German battalion there

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:32.800
for about 24 hours.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:36.130
General John Lucas sat
there for five days

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:39.612
until the Germans made an entire
semicircle of heavy artillery

00:24:39.612 --> 00:24:40.570
and armor against them.

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:41.860
Patton would not have done.

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:43.510
He was not given
either appointment

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:47.380
because he was under suspicion
for slapping two soldiers.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:51.070
He was not saved, as is
often seen in the movie

00:24:51.070 --> 00:24:54.110
and in popular literature,
by Omar Bradley,

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:55.300
who wanted him sent home.

00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:57.820
He was not saved
necessarily by Eisenhower.

00:24:57.820 --> 00:25:00.860
His letters of support
were post-facto.

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:02.530
It was not even George Marshall.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:05.470
It was Henry Stimson,
secretary of war,

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:08.290
who said that we can't win
the war without this man,

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:12.940
or at least we can't win it
at a cost that we can afford.

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:16.540
So then we come to the
big command's decision,

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:18.790
the planning of the
invasion of Normandy,

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:21.760
that had been talked
about since 1942.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:23.920
Americans under Marshall
had told the British,

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:26.590
we want to invade
November of 1942.

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:28.990
Even they, after
they admitted what

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:31.870
had happened in North Africa,
thought that was a mistake.

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:34.150
And then we wanted to go in '43.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.130
And then after our
problems in Sicily,

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:37.190
they said, that was why.

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:39.310
So then we wanted to
go in January of '44.

00:25:39.310 --> 00:25:41.500
Then they said, you're
right, to the Brits.

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:42.940
We had trouble in Italy.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:46.120
Patton had said that all along.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:51.040
He was completely shut out
of all planning for Overlord.

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:53.650
In fact, it was adding
insult to injury

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:56.980
where he was in command
of a shadow army, what

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:00.190
the Germans called Army Group
Patton, where he just raced

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:02.760
around in a car all up
and down from Scotland all

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:06.220
the way to Wales with horn
blaring so everybody thought he

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:07.810
was going to invade at Calais.

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:10.150
And then four Panzer
divisions would

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:13.330
be held in reserve, waiting
for Invasion Patton, which

00:26:13.330 --> 00:26:14.980
never would come.

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:17.380
And then their Panzers would
not be released to cut off

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:19.120
the American beachhead.

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:23.090
So he didn't
participate in Normandy.

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:26.050
Remember, after that
brilliant landing,

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:28.630
at least outside
of Omaha Beach, we

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:33.220
were ossified, calcified
for about 40 days.

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:34.870
We lost more in
the next two weeks

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:39.010
than we did in the
landing, and we eventually

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:43.360
had 80,000 casualties before we
broke out of [INAUDIBLE] July

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:45.610
28 with Operation Cobra.

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:50.080
Even then, when Patton's
Third Army was operational,

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:52.540
Bradley tried to stop the
publicity of the release

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:54.980
of news accounts of it.

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:58.510
I'm not suggesting that he
was mild-mannered and polite

00:26:58.510 --> 00:26:59.710
about this.

00:26:59.710 --> 00:27:01.990
He said things that
were absolutely insane.

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:05.590
He said, Brad, just point
me in the right direction

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:08.507
and you're not going
to even see me.

00:27:08.507 --> 00:27:10.090
I'll be gone all the
way to the Rhine.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:12.590
Said things like
that all the time.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:17.350
But again, the point was to
take a civilian conscript group

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:20.530
of young men and make them into
an army that could stand up

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:22.690
to people who had been
fighting on the Eastern

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:26.230
Front for four years against the
toughest soldiers in the world.

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:29.770
When you start to look at
some of those German armored

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:33.190
divisions, Panzer
Lehr division, and you

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:34.510
see the type of equipment--

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:39.040
88-millimeter Panther Tiger
1 and 2 tanks-- it's amazing

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.080
that we could stand up to them.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:42.850
And that was Patton's idea.

00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:48.840
So they assigned him the
furthest south position

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:50.440
in the advance toward Germany.

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:53.260
And if you take a map
and draw a line on it,

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:55.330
it would go into
Czechoslovakia, not Germany.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:56.260
That was by intent.

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:59.020
But most importantly,
they ask him

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.855
to take Brest, which if you
look at the map from Normandy,

00:28:01.855 --> 00:28:06.220
he would have to turn around
and go the opposite direction.

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:10.780
And he was not designated to
go really, in the first month,

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:11.600
east at all.

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:13.308
Of course, you know
Patton-- what he did.

00:28:13.308 --> 00:28:15.760
He took one third of
his army and went north,

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:19.480
and he sent one west,
and then he headed east.

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:23.310
And in a series of orders
that were overridden,

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:30.190
he said to General John Wood
[INAUDIBLE],, take Brest now.

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:32.710
And they were a day away,
and Bradley stopped it.

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:35.110
And of course, you know
what happened after that.

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:37.810
Brest was heavily fortified.

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:40.430
It took us about two months.

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:42.910
We had about 40,000
casualties, and the port

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:45.110
was never used until October.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:47.230
It was a complete waste of time.

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:52.560
When he did go east, he soon
was able, after the break

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.910
out, to go about 40 miles a day.

00:28:54.910 --> 00:29:01.000
He went 400 miles by the middle
of first week in September.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.640
When we got to Argentan
and the Falaise Pocket,

00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:06.570
there was the entire
Army Group West.

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:11.740
300,000 Germans were
advancing toward the Atlantic,

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:14.860
while Patton was coming
up to the rear in hopes

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.120
of meeting the Canadians.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:18.940
And Hitler would not
let Von Kluge retreat

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:20.830
and it looked like
the war would be over.

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:24.610
All we had to do was
close the Falaise Pocket.

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:27.280
And Omar Bradley
said, you will not

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:30.550
do that because I'd
rather have a round

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:32.170
shoulder than a broken neck.

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:35.710
We have to have an escape
route for some of them.

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:37.540
And Patton said, oh, hell.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:38.837
I'll meet the Canadians.

00:29:38.837 --> 00:29:39.670
I'll meet the Brits.

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:41.170
And if the Brits
get in my way, I'll

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:44.070
push them all the
way to Dunkirk.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:45.440
You can't say that.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.740
And so Monty will
never close it.

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:50.830
Well, it was never
closed for seven days.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:51.850
It was heavily bombed.

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:53.330
10,000 Germans were killed.

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:55.900
Most of the equipment was lost.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:30:01.960
But about 155,000 Germans
escaped, about 10 divisions,

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:04.660
2 of which were re-armored
and we dropped--

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:09.340
Montgomery did-- the 101st,
the 82nd, and the 1st British

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:11.350
Parachute Division
right on top of them

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:14.740
basically in the
Arnhem Market Garden.

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:19.660
So when General Model defeated
us and destroyed Monty's plan

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:21.670
to leapfrog in,
most of those troops

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:23.680
had been in the Falaise Pocket.

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:26.170
When he got past
Paris, he begged

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:29.500
to close another gap,
another encirclement,

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:32.290
on the eastern side
of the Seine River.

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:33.380
He was not listened to.

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:35.680
He said, he had too few troops.

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:38.080
And then he headed
back into Lorraine.

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:43.350
And on September 2, his daily
allotment-- think of this--

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:48.070
400,000 gallons of gasoline,
he got 15,000 gallons.

00:30:48.070 --> 00:30:51.310
Part of it was
because the operation

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:53.320
was conceptually flawed.

00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:57.100
There was not enough supplies
to get from the beaches

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:01.930
300 or 400 miles into
Germany and support

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:04.000
about 700,000 troops--

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:05.470
Canadian, American, and British.

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:08.120
And part was that
Eisenhower had made,

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:11.590
I think, a disastrous
decision by redirecting

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:14.920
most of the supplies of
the US 3rd and 1st Army

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:19.150
in the month of September
1944 to Montgomery

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:20.980
to do this Market
Garden bit when

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:27.070
he didn't close the estuaries
at the harbor at Antwerp.

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:29.170
Had we got Antwerp, nobody
would have had problems

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:29.830
with supplies.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:33.700
The net result is Patton
stayed 45 days without fuel

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:37.510
and ended up in a World
War I slugfest, which

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:40.060
wasn't his forte, around Metz.

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:44.080
And finally, in March, when
he went across the Rhine

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:46.540
with the 1st Army to
his North, the rest

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:47.770
is pretty much history.

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:50.380
He captured about
350,000 prisoners,

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:52.880
and he went 300
miles in two weeks,

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:54.310
and that was the end of the war.

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:59.830
He did tell Eisenhower,
I can take Berlin.

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.210
And Ike said, we're
not gonna lose 300,000.

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:04.570
That's how much we think
the Russians will lose.

00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:06.573
They lost 170,000 men.

00:32:06.573 --> 00:32:08.490
And he said something
that was very prescient.

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.670
He said, they will
lose that many,

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:12.430
but when the Germans
see Americans coming,

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:15.370
they'll be all too
happy to surrender to us

00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:18.310
and not go into
gulags or be killed.

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:21.040
And he was not allowed
to take Berlin,

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:23.990
and I think that
had repercussions.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:27.220
So all of these decisions that
I talked about where I think

00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:30.760
he was right had repercussions.

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:33.940
We lost an inordinate
amount of people in Italy.

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:35.860
We never got into Austria.

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:38.650
The whole idea of
Churchill's soft underbelly

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:40.990
going into Europe or going
through the Ljubljana Gap

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:43.450
was a failure.

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:46.840
When the war ended, American
troops were still in Italy.

00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:50.050
I don't know what the purpose
of the Sicily campaign was.

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:52.840
It was to be a stepping
stone into Italy.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:55.690
You go through history all
the way back to Hannibal,

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:59.290
and no army has ever
invaded Sicily and gone--

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:02.380
invaded Italy and gone
into northern Europe

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:04.780
successfully by taking Sicily.

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:06.760
You usually have to go
to Sardinia or Corsica

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:09.370
and go across, or cut off
somebody, or go from the north,

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:10.900
south.

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:15.160
So those entire campaigns,
I think, were flawed.

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:18.310
The Normandy
campaign, I think, we

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:21.400
could have said it could have
been over in September 1944

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.460
had we closed the gap, or had
we tried to close it again

00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:26.290
at the Seine.

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:27.790
His greatest moment,
of course, was

00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:29.470
during the Battle of the Bulge.

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:33.010
Patton was finally
given gasoline.

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:35.020
He was on the offensive.

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:39.100
And-- called the Von
Runstedt Offensive,

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:40.570
but it was not really his idea.

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:43.930
It was Hitler's mad idea that
we had two green divisions

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:53.890
in the 1st Army and if he took
a 250,000-man salient and broke

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.560
through, he could separate the
Americans from the British.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:58.540
There would not be
overall command.

00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:00.940
He could go to the
Meuse River, and then

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:04.270
maybe, just maybe go
to Antwerp, and then

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.880
have some type of private deal
with the British or us and say,

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:07.840
let's just call it off.

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:10.690
But he exhausted
most of the reserves.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:15.370
The problem was if you were
one of 50,000 US troops in that

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:20.320
pathway, and you woke up for 10
days every morning and it was

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:23.230
snowy and you had
no air support,

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:27.340
when 90% of the aircraft in the
sky for the last 60 days had

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:30.760
been American or British,
and suddenly you were looking

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:33.550
at battle-hard veterans
and they had, as I said,

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:37.030
Tigers and Panthers, you were
gonna die or be captured--

00:34:37.030 --> 00:34:39.130
80,000 Americans are.

00:34:39.130 --> 00:34:40.853
Patton saw that happening.

00:34:40.853 --> 00:34:43.270
And when he went to the meeting
with Eisenhower at Verdun,

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:44.645
he knew what was
going to happen,

00:34:44.645 --> 00:34:47.260
that somebody had to
cut off the salient.

00:34:47.260 --> 00:34:50.350
He had already told
his staff, we've

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:52.630
got to turn around
three entire divisions.

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:54.699
I mean, that's 45,000
men, and turn them

00:34:54.699 --> 00:34:56.600
in the opposite
direction in 24 hours.

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:58.497
So it was his greatest moment.

00:34:58.497 --> 00:34:59.455
It was very theatrical.

00:34:59.455 --> 00:35:01.210
He went to the meeting
and Eisenhower said,

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:02.970
I don't know how we're
going to stop these guys.

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:04.262
They're going to take Bastogne.

00:35:04.262 --> 00:35:05.930
They may get to the Meuse.

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:10.000
And he announced to
Bradley, and to Montgomery,

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:14.132
and Eisenhower, I can
turn three divisions

00:35:14.132 --> 00:35:15.090
and I can cut them off.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:16.923
And he said, George,
that'll take two weeks.

00:35:16.923 --> 00:35:20.178
And he said, I'll go out and
give the signal on the phone

00:35:20.178 --> 00:35:21.970
and they will start in
24 hours, and that's

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:23.120
exactly what they did.

00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:26.800
The tragedy of that is, of
course, he argued vehemently

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:31.540
that the way to stop a bulge
is not to go at a diagonal

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:34.840
and push the nose back,
it's to take a chance

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:38.320
and cut it off at the
base and allow a few more

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:40.600
casualties at the
tip, but therefore,

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:42.220
destroy the entire invasion.

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:44.080
Of course, that was
considered too reckless,

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:45.182
and we didn't do that.

00:35:45.182 --> 00:35:47.140
And that battle went all
the way into February,

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:50.950
and we lost more men after
Bastogne than before.

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:53.950
What I'm getting at is
that there's a pattern here

00:35:53.950 --> 00:35:58.180
of somebody that has undeniable
experience, preparation,

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:00.760
and natural genius,
who understands

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:05.830
the horrific nature of war, and
bothers the people that command

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:09.880
him, and yet, sequentially
or time and time

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:15.490
again, when he is not given
a billet, or an appointment,

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:18.760
or a promotion befitting what
he's earned on the battlefield,

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:20.830
people die.

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:26.230
And yet the way that the system
or the therapeutic society

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:29.590
justifies that is that
he slapped a soldier.

00:36:29.590 --> 00:36:31.630
Germans, of course,
were bewildered by this.

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:34.135
I mean, it is a little mythical
the Germans knew of Patton.

00:36:34.135 --> 00:36:36.010
I don't think the movie's
quite right that he

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:38.590
was canonized by the Germans.

00:36:38.590 --> 00:36:41.437
Mostly after the war, they
sort of changed their views.

00:36:41.437 --> 00:36:43.270
They call him a cowboy
and kind of reckless.

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:45.670
But the point I'm making
is that it is true

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:47.950
that a number of German
intelligence officers

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:52.000
wouldn't believe that
somebody of his talent

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:54.940
would be relieved for slapping
a couple soldiers when

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:58.300
German officers killed 25,000
soldiers in World War II,

00:36:58.300 --> 00:37:02.380
shot them for cowardice
or had them ordered shot.

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:06.100
And so this brings up
finally, to conclude,

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:08.410
I entitled his talk
"The Tragic Nature,"

00:37:08.410 --> 00:37:11.440
and I think Patton is
symbolic of a problem

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:15.040
that all Western societies
deal with, since the Greeks,

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.150
that the advent of civilization
is a wonderful thing.

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:19.720
It creates leisure.

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:22.730
It creates material
wealth, luxury.

00:37:22.730 --> 00:37:24.290
It's civilization.

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:25.240
It's not tribalism.

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:26.590
It's not barbarism.

00:37:26.590 --> 00:37:30.820
But in that process,
we become tame.

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:33.850
And yet, the world
around us is not tame,

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:37.900
and we don't quite know how
to justify using violence

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:39.550
against people who
want to kill us.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:42.490
This sounds very contemporarily
relevant, I think all of you

00:37:42.490 --> 00:37:44.290
know, in the war against terror.

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:48.010
And so from time to time, we
see these fossilized memories

00:37:48.010 --> 00:37:50.770
of our past and we bring them
out of the proverbial closet

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:53.020
and we say, help us.

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:56.530
Curtis LeMay, the B-29
program doesn't work.

00:37:56.530 --> 00:37:59.170
I know it's safe for our
flyers at 30,000 feet,

00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:02.740
but the bombs are
falling off [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:04.990
Well, you go down
5,000 with napalm.

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:06.430
That'll cure it.

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:07.330
Oh, my God.

00:38:07.330 --> 00:38:09.670
You're going to
burn people alive?

00:38:09.670 --> 00:38:11.710
I'll get rid of the industry.

00:38:11.710 --> 00:38:13.007
Or Matthew Ridgway.

00:38:13.007 --> 00:38:14.590
I'm going to let him
come in, and then

00:38:14.590 --> 00:38:16.060
I'm going to surround
them with napalm,

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:18.310
and I'm going blast them,
and it's going to be winter,

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:21.810
and they're going to regret
they ever went into Korea.

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:23.770
Or Sherman.

00:38:23.770 --> 00:38:25.900
This is the plantation
of Howell Cobb?

00:38:25.900 --> 00:38:30.550
This is the guy who said that
250,000 Confederate soldiers

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:31.710
were superior to us?

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:33.580
Burn his plantation.

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:37.990
[INAUDIBLE] he burned
a Southern plantation.

00:38:37.990 --> 00:38:41.290
And so when we see
somebody like Patton--

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:44.092
and you can see
it in our culture,

00:38:44.092 --> 00:38:45.050
throughout our culture.

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:46.390
It's just not military.

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:49.510
That was what made
John Ford famous,

00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:51.910
that if you're Ethan Allen--

00:38:51.910 --> 00:38:54.070
Ethan Edwards, excuse
me-- in The Searchers

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:56.272
and you want to
find a small girl,

00:38:56.272 --> 00:38:57.730
and you're dealing
with some pretty

00:38:57.730 --> 00:39:00.520
tough Native
American tribes, you

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:02.500
want somebody with
a dubious past.

00:39:02.500 --> 00:39:05.170
We're not quite told what he
was, maybe a Quantrill Raider--

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:06.340
John Wayne.

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:09.550
And you don't know whether
he's going to kill Natalie Wood

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:12.550
or not, but he has the skills
that both ensure that he's

00:39:12.550 --> 00:39:13.550
going to bring her back.

00:39:13.550 --> 00:39:16.300
But as you remember, once
she's back, he opens the door

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:16.930
and walks out.

00:39:16.930 --> 00:39:19.120
You don't want a
guy like that there,

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:22.960
any more than you want
Gary Cooper in High Noon

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:26.180
to stay around after he's
done what you have to do,

00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:28.270
shoot four people in the street.

00:39:28.270 --> 00:39:31.730
You don't want the Magnificent
Seven in the village anymore.

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:36.040
Remember that famous Yul
Brynner and Steve McQueen said,

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:39.645
well, I guess they're happy.

00:39:39.645 --> 00:39:41.770
And he said, they'll even
be happier when we leave.

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:43.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:47.200
And I'll just finish by saying,
this is not a new phenomenon,

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:52.120
that we sometimes
misdiagnose talent

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:55.300
throughout all aspects
of American society.

00:39:55.300 --> 00:39:56.320
I did make a promise--

00:39:56.320 --> 00:39:57.695
I just want to
interrupt-- that I

00:39:57.695 --> 00:40:01.300
wasn't going to mention the
name Donald Trump in this talk.

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:03.850
But you can see
that this person has

00:40:03.850 --> 00:40:06.670
certain obnoxious
characteristics

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:09.820
and certain skill sets
that bring results.

00:40:09.820 --> 00:40:11.830
And you can guarantee
that after we

00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:14.200
are the beneficiaries
of the results,

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:16.510
he's not going to be
on PBS in 10 years

00:40:16.510 --> 00:40:18.650
with ex-presidents
shooting the breeze.

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:20.420
It's just not going to happen.

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:22.810
He's going to be
persona non grata.

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:25.270
And I'll just finish by--

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:31.120
when pre-civilized Greece
was making this transition

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:32.830
to the city-state,
and especially

00:40:32.830 --> 00:40:35.560
to radical democracy,
there were people

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:37.130
who saw the same phenomenon.

00:40:37.130 --> 00:40:40.600
One of the great minds of
the Western literary canon,

00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:41.970
Sophocles--

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:43.750
he wrote 113 plays.

00:40:43.750 --> 00:40:44.890
We have seven extant.

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:46.540
But in a series of
plays, he looked

00:40:46.540 --> 00:40:50.680
at this archetype of the
oligarchic aristocratic class

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:54.340
that we had all of these
anti-democratic skills,

00:40:54.340 --> 00:40:55.275
and you needed them.

00:40:55.275 --> 00:40:57.400
And it was right in the
middle, some of the plays--

00:40:57.400 --> 00:40:59.233
the latter plays were
the Peloponnesian War.

00:40:59.233 --> 00:41:02.650
I'm thinking here of
Philoctetes, Ajax, Antigone.

00:41:02.650 --> 00:41:05.260
But he makes these
characters come out,

00:41:05.260 --> 00:41:08.230
and by every measure
of talent, and courage,

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:10.990
and bravery, they excel.

00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:14.290
And yet, they all end up
badly, because to reward them

00:41:14.290 --> 00:41:18.220
for those very characteristics
would be a referendum

00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:19.760
on your own society.

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:22.285
And I think that's a dilemma
we all have to appreciate.

00:41:22.285 --> 00:41:24.320
I'm not asking us
to change our views,

00:41:24.320 --> 00:41:27.030
but to, every once in a
while, look in the corners.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:28.780
And when we see dark
people, maybe they're

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:29.987
not so dark after all.

00:41:29.987 --> 00:41:30.820
Thank you very much.

00:41:30.820 --> 00:41:34.700
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:47.420
SPEAKER 1: Thank
you, Dr. Hanson.

00:41:47.420 --> 00:41:49.647
We now have time
for a few questions.

00:41:49.647 --> 00:41:51.230
Please make your way
to the microphone

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:54.510
if you have a question.

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:56.350
SPEAKER 2: Yes?

00:41:56.350 --> 00:41:58.100
AUDIENCE: Dr. Hanson,
thank you very much.

00:41:58.100 --> 00:41:59.900
Excellent presentation.

00:41:59.900 --> 00:42:02.330
The one element of
Patton that I've always

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:05.970
wanted to get more information
on is right before his death,

00:42:05.970 --> 00:42:09.710
he got into more trouble because
he wanted to invade Russia.

00:42:09.710 --> 00:42:12.860
And he wanted to arm
the Germans in the camps

00:42:12.860 --> 00:42:14.840
and push all the way to
Moscow because he said,

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:18.560
it'll avoid problems
in the future.

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:21.062
Can you comment on it?

00:42:21.062 --> 00:42:22.520
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Well, remember

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:26.360
that I think his name was--

00:42:26.360 --> 00:42:27.710
they called him Jeep Sanza.

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:33.170
His regular chauffeur had been
transferred home after the war,

00:42:33.170 --> 00:42:36.350
so he had a new driver, and the
new driver wasn't as skilled.

00:42:36.350 --> 00:42:38.690
And the three people who
were with the driver--

00:42:38.690 --> 00:42:40.490
there were Patton
and two others--

00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:42.420
they had superficial injuries.

00:42:42.420 --> 00:42:45.350
So the fact that
he hit the glass

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:47.900
partition was kind of
a freak accident and he

00:42:47.900 --> 00:42:49.350
was on his way home.

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:52.730
The reason that these conspiracy
theories arose was because--

00:42:52.730 --> 00:42:53.300
two things.

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:56.390
He'd been relieved by
Eisenhower for a phone

00:42:56.390 --> 00:42:59.970
call he had with Walter Bedell
Smith, and in it, he said,

00:42:59.970 --> 00:43:00.470
my God.

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:03.090
We've got the people here.

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:04.700
They're all over here.

00:43:04.700 --> 00:43:07.250
And the Russians
are natural killers,

00:43:07.250 --> 00:43:10.670
and we went to war, supposedly
to free Eastern Europe

00:43:10.670 --> 00:43:13.110
from Nazis, and now we're
going to enslave it.

00:43:13.110 --> 00:43:14.360
So he said stuff.

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:17.450
I don't know how serious he
was, but that helped get him--

00:43:17.450 --> 00:43:19.310
and then he said another thing.

00:43:19.310 --> 00:43:21.890
They said, you've got too
many Nazis working for you.

00:43:21.890 --> 00:43:24.107
And he says, they're like
Democrats and Republicans.

00:43:24.107 --> 00:43:25.940
When Republicans come
in, they can't get rid

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:28.310
of all the Democrats out
of the civil service.

00:43:28.310 --> 00:43:30.480
How can I get rid of these guys?

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:33.200
And he said it in such
a way that was callous.

00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:44.250
So his main argument was
that Bolshevism, communism,

00:43:44.250 --> 00:43:48.330
after the war would be
as existential a threat

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:50.850
to the United States
as Nazi Germany,

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:53.940
but he had greater respect--

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:56.400
not admiration, but respect--

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:59.040
for Russian military
prowess and capability

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:02.080
than Germany because of their
population and industry.

00:44:02.080 --> 00:44:04.830
So he was terrified
of T-34 tanks

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:07.470
and the ruthlessness
of the Soviet Army.

00:44:07.470 --> 00:44:10.440
They had, remember,
lost 11 million soldiers

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:13.020
and 9 million civilians--

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:14.640
20 million people.

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:17.070
And he thought any
regime that could

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:20.890
survive that was terrifying.

00:44:20.890 --> 00:44:23.850
So I think he was
right in the diagnosis,

00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:26.730
but America was in no
psychological position

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:29.340
in May, when we were still
fighting the Japanese.

00:44:29.340 --> 00:44:31.260
And we were still a
nominal ally of Russia,

00:44:31.260 --> 00:44:33.870
and we were trying to
flatter the Russians--

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:37.020
we didn't know if the bomb was
going to work, or if it worked,

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:38.190
it would bring a surrender.

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:41.622
And we didn't believe LeMay
could end it through air power,

00:44:41.622 --> 00:44:43.830
so we thought we were going
to have to get the Soviet

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:48.800
Union to help us.

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:50.490
AUDIENCE: Dr. Hanson,
you talked a bit

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.650
about the therapeutic
society that we live in

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:54.990
and you gave a few examples
in more recent times

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:56.740
about how it seems to
have advanced a bit.

00:44:56.740 --> 00:44:58.800
Do you think it could
get to the point--

00:44:58.800 --> 00:45:01.020
Patton himself, you gave
many examples in which

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:03.338
he was ridiculed and attacked.

00:45:03.338 --> 00:45:05.130
Do you think it could
ever get to the point

00:45:05.130 --> 00:45:07.047
where we would turn to
someone like him again?

00:45:10.990 --> 00:45:14.230
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Well,
in the first Gulf War,

00:45:14.230 --> 00:45:15.940
you could see that Arnold--

00:45:15.940 --> 00:45:18.010
I mean, General Schwarzenegger--

00:45:18.010 --> 00:45:19.870
thought he was going
to play that role.

00:45:19.870 --> 00:45:21.810
You remember?

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:24.680
Not Schwarzenegger.

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:29.560
I've been hanging around
Al Phillip and my Austrian.

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:34.090
But there were people
in the first Gulf War

00:45:34.090 --> 00:45:38.090
and the second Gulf War that
wanted to play that role.

00:45:38.090 --> 00:45:41.860
But if you look at
both those wars,

00:45:41.860 --> 00:45:43.870
after the so-called
Highway of Death,

00:45:43.870 --> 00:45:47.140
there wasn't really a highway
of death, as the media said.

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:49.810
And then during the so-called
armistice with Saddam,

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:51.640
we let them have helicopters.

00:45:51.640 --> 00:45:53.640
They butchered--
it was a disaster.

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:59.590
We didn't have anybody who said,
the more the Imperial Guard

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:02.292
is given a reprieve,
the more innocent people

00:46:02.292 --> 00:46:04.000
are going to be killed,
and therefore, we

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:04.960
have to destroy them.

00:46:04.960 --> 00:46:06.700
We just didn't do that.

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:09.610
So it's getting harder
as a society becomes

00:46:09.610 --> 00:46:13.510
more leisured and more
materially enriched

00:46:13.510 --> 00:46:16.025
to find people from more
pre-civilized paths,

00:46:16.025 --> 00:46:17.150
because that's what war is.

00:46:17.150 --> 00:46:19.150
It's pre-civilized.

00:46:19.150 --> 00:46:21.140
And so I kind of
worry about that.

00:46:21.140 --> 00:46:27.510
And we're all experiencing
a war that started in 2001.

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:29.700
It's been 19 years
in Afghanistan.

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:33.430
We're told by this whole array
of experts all the reasons

00:46:33.430 --> 00:46:34.280
that we have--

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:36.120
and I supported the initial war.

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:36.940
But I counted.

00:46:36.940 --> 00:46:37.940
I wrote an article once.

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:39.910
There were 23 different
reasons I was told

00:46:39.910 --> 00:46:41.770
we had to be in Afghanistan.

00:46:41.770 --> 00:46:43.440
Nobody said we're
going to win the war,

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:46.660
and there's no
substitute for victory.

00:46:46.660 --> 00:46:50.360
So I don't know what
Libya was about.

00:46:50.360 --> 00:46:52.900
I don't know what
the Syria thing was.

00:46:52.900 --> 00:46:56.480
Iraq, we kind of won the
war, but then we pulled out.

00:46:56.480 --> 00:46:57.520
So it's hard.

00:46:57.520 --> 00:47:01.090
It's very hard to see that.

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:02.020
It's not the generals.

00:47:02.020 --> 00:47:04.900
The generals are
representations of us,

00:47:04.900 --> 00:47:07.870
and there were people coming
out of the Depression who

00:47:07.870 --> 00:47:10.660
were impoverished that
didn't have the luxury

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:11.780
to be therapeutic.

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:14.480
And so Patton was the
most popular general.

00:47:14.480 --> 00:47:16.030
The reason he was
so successful was

00:47:16.030 --> 00:47:18.370
he had broad public support.

00:47:18.370 --> 00:47:21.640
The parents of the soldier
he slapped wrote a letter

00:47:21.640 --> 00:47:24.280
and said that, I think it
was wrong what you did,

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:26.560
but boy, we're not
going to criticize you.

00:47:26.560 --> 00:47:28.030
You're saving lives.

00:47:28.030 --> 00:47:30.250
I just don't think that
would happen again.

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:32.092
So the general today--

00:47:32.092 --> 00:47:34.300
and I think General Petraeus
was a very good general,

00:47:34.300 --> 00:47:36.970
but it's more of an
intellectual with a PhD,

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:39.940
rather than a blood-and-guts
type of person.

00:47:39.940 --> 00:47:42.250
I don't think you'll see
people like Grant or Sherman

00:47:42.250 --> 00:47:44.610
again, or Lee.

00:47:44.610 --> 00:47:48.010
I think if a
general said today--

00:47:48.010 --> 00:47:52.330
just imagine the new
theater commander in CENTCOM

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:53.650
went over to Afghanistan.

00:47:53.650 --> 00:47:55.150
He had a press
conference, said, I'm

00:47:55.150 --> 00:47:57.410
going to beat those
sons of bitches.

00:47:57.410 --> 00:47:59.860
They say, the Taliban?

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:00.970
Sons of bitches?

00:48:00.970 --> 00:48:02.530
That's too simplistic.

00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:05.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:08.652
So I just don't think
it's going to happen.

00:48:08.652 --> 00:48:09.977
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:09.977 --> 00:48:11.060
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: What?

00:48:13.088 --> 00:48:14.880
AUDIENCE: Dr. Hanson,
thank you for coming.

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:15.963
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Yeah.

00:48:15.963 --> 00:48:16.607
Question?

00:48:16.607 --> 00:48:19.190
AUDIENCE: And I'd also like to
say on behalf of my father, who

00:48:19.190 --> 00:48:20.565
specifically told
me to tell you,

00:48:20.565 --> 00:48:23.875
that he's read just about
every word you've ever written.

00:48:23.875 --> 00:48:25.250
But I specifically
wanted to ask,

00:48:25.250 --> 00:48:29.060
sort of like you talked
about how Patton was clearly

00:48:29.060 --> 00:48:31.300
strategically sound in
a lot of his thinking,

00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:34.315
and seeing what was happening
on the Eastern Front, and just

00:48:34.315 --> 00:48:35.690
the brutality of
the Nazis, is we

00:48:35.690 --> 00:48:37.670
got to go in and make
the other poor bastard

00:48:37.670 --> 00:48:39.150
die for his country.

00:48:39.150 --> 00:48:43.250
So like you talk about, just
feeling democratic societies

00:48:43.250 --> 00:48:45.470
get kind of soft.

00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:49.940
Was that so ingrained and
the Allied high command

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:52.130
having to play all the
politics of the reasons,

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:54.620
and the fact that
he was abrasive--

00:48:58.450 --> 00:48:58.970
yeah, sorry.

00:48:58.970 --> 00:49:05.790
So why exactly was the fact that
he was clearly strategically

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:08.550
sound putting away?

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:12.180
Was it really just
the fact that he

00:49:12.180 --> 00:49:14.365
made people a little
uncomfortable because he

00:49:14.365 --> 00:49:15.990
acknowledged that we
were going to have

00:49:15.990 --> 00:49:19.050
to be violent to win the war?

00:49:19.050 --> 00:49:21.420
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Well,
we know from his own War

00:49:21.420 --> 00:49:24.540
As I Knew It that he
made faces in the mirror.

00:49:24.540 --> 00:49:28.560
A lot of it was show,
and they weren't pearl.

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:30.390
They were ivory-handled guns.

00:49:30.390 --> 00:49:33.810
He had that weird horn on
his Jeep with the placards,

00:49:33.810 --> 00:49:35.260
polished his helmet.

00:49:35.260 --> 00:49:38.700
All of that was an image
to show people that--

00:49:41.220 --> 00:49:43.965
he'd give these speeches.

00:49:43.965 --> 00:49:45.840
Nobody ever won a war
by dying for a country.

00:49:45.840 --> 00:49:47.580
You kill the SOB, not dying.

00:49:47.580 --> 00:49:49.900
And he gave a terrible speech.

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:52.080
It may be effective, but
it was frightening when

00:49:52.080 --> 00:49:54.540
he said, all of you wounded--

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:56.250
this was in Boston,
of all places--

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:58.833
all of you should be very happy
because you didn't get killed.

00:49:58.833 --> 00:50:00.840
People get killed never did--

00:50:00.840 --> 00:50:03.600
just trying to create
this image of himself

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:05.880
and the American Army
as the equivalent

00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:09.000
on the battlefield of the
Germans and the Russians.

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:11.405
And he had a lot of
distinct disadvantages

00:50:11.405 --> 00:50:12.530
of doing that in a society.

00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:17.850
And then when he
went out of character

00:50:17.850 --> 00:50:21.660
at the Lorraine Campaign and
Metz, he didn't have fuel

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:26.250
and he had, as I said, a World
War I stagnant objective,

00:50:26.250 --> 00:50:28.270
he didn't do very well.

00:50:28.270 --> 00:50:32.370
So his idea was the
Americans are growing up.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:33.390
They're young people.

00:50:33.390 --> 00:50:34.770
Everybody works on a car.

00:50:34.770 --> 00:50:35.430
Germans don't.

00:50:35.430 --> 00:50:36.710
Russians don't.

00:50:36.710 --> 00:50:40.770
1942, everybody knew how to
take apart a Model A or a Chevy.

00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:42.390
They like their frontier image.

00:50:42.390 --> 00:50:43.140
They're horsemen.

00:50:43.140 --> 00:50:44.160
They like to move.

00:50:44.160 --> 00:50:47.490
So you've got to adopt
those natural tendencies

00:50:47.490 --> 00:50:50.910
and have armor outflanking
thrusts with air support.

00:50:50.910 --> 00:50:52.230
Forget the flanks.

00:50:52.230 --> 00:50:53.160
Circle around.

00:50:53.160 --> 00:50:54.120
Confuse the enemy.

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:56.070
Don't lose a lot of people.

00:50:56.070 --> 00:50:56.700
Bam, bam.

00:50:56.700 --> 00:51:00.420
And he tried to craft a
strategic doctrine of war

00:51:00.420 --> 00:51:02.850
that he felt was compatible
with the American character

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:04.260
as far as he could do it.

00:51:04.260 --> 00:51:05.820
I think he did a
pretty good job.

00:51:05.820 --> 00:51:10.710
In comparison to people
that had no idea of this.

00:51:10.710 --> 00:51:12.120
Hodges had no idea of this.

00:51:12.120 --> 00:51:14.970
Mark Clark would
never think like this.

00:51:14.970 --> 00:51:16.610
Bradley would never think.

00:51:16.610 --> 00:51:18.180
Eisenhower's chief
worry is, how do I

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:19.540
keep the coalition together?

00:51:19.540 --> 00:51:20.970
He did a good job.

00:51:20.970 --> 00:51:23.580
But they'd always say--

00:51:23.580 --> 00:51:26.580
when Patton said, I can
move three divisions

00:51:26.580 --> 00:51:31.860
and save Bastogne, Eisenhower
said, careful now, Patton.

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:34.050
Your reputation hasn't
been too good lately.

00:51:34.050 --> 00:51:36.540
He said, I think
it's pretty good.

00:51:36.540 --> 00:51:38.910
Can you imagine the Supreme
Commander telling that

00:51:38.910 --> 00:51:43.380
to somebody who's just offered a
way to save an entire division?

00:51:43.380 --> 00:51:46.770
So it was constant
and it wore him out.

00:51:46.770 --> 00:51:51.000
It was constant ankle-biting,
bureaucratic, deep state.

00:51:51.000 --> 00:51:55.620
And he, himself, was a throwback
to a pre-civilized mentality,

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:59.190
an aristocratic mentality where
he felt that rich people who

00:51:59.190 --> 00:52:02.490
were well-educated and knew
how to ride horses, and play

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:06.990
polo, and sail should be
given certain latitudes.

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:08.880
And that was an anathema
to a guy who grew up

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:10.300
really poor, like Omar Bradley.

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:16.870
AUDIENCE: Thank you for joining
us again on campus, Dr. Hanson.

00:52:16.870 --> 00:52:18.370
Throughout this
series, I can't help

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:20.830
but wonder how
the same qualities

00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:24.640
seem to make a person
both famous and infamous.

00:52:24.640 --> 00:52:27.790
I'm curious if this is
perhaps a curse of success

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:29.590
or just a factor
of human nature.

00:52:32.703 --> 00:52:34.120
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Well, I think

00:52:34.120 --> 00:52:37.970
you're quite correct in
your general observation.

00:52:37.970 --> 00:52:43.240
Now I'm quoting the 7th
century BC poet Hesiod.

00:52:43.240 --> 00:52:45.400
"The most powerful
of all human emotions

00:52:45.400 --> 00:52:49.840
is envy," what they
called [INAUDIBLE]..

00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:52.600
And it's true that the
more successful person--

00:52:52.600 --> 00:52:55.240
that's what Greek ostracism and
the democratic culture created.

00:52:55.240 --> 00:52:57.490
Ostracize somebody not because
he did something wrong,

00:52:57.490 --> 00:52:59.320
because everybody
knows who he is.

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:01.300
So we don't like
people who do things

00:53:01.300 --> 00:53:04.380
that we can't in a democratic,
pluralistic society.

00:53:04.380 --> 00:53:08.800
But I think in this
case, we have a sense, as

00:53:08.800 --> 00:53:12.400
a democratic, affluent,
21st century--

00:53:12.400 --> 00:53:14.580
I think Barack Obama
summed it up best.

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:18.580
He always used this term, the
arc of history is bending.

00:53:18.580 --> 00:53:21.680
And it was always as if things
are getting better, better,

00:53:21.680 --> 00:53:24.550
better, and we're going to
get more humane and humane.

00:53:24.550 --> 00:53:26.380
And we're changing human nation.

00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:28.180
We have more colleges.

00:53:28.180 --> 00:53:30.580
There's more kids
in the Ivy League.

00:53:30.580 --> 00:53:32.020
We've got all this technology.

00:53:32.020 --> 00:53:34.420
And we're getting better,
and better, and better.

00:53:34.420 --> 00:53:38.050
And we're going to eliminate
people like Patton.

00:53:38.050 --> 00:53:40.160
And I don't think we
are getting better.

00:53:40.160 --> 00:53:43.600
I don't think there's any proof
in history that we all are--

00:53:43.600 --> 00:53:45.850
other than technology, and
even that is up and down--

00:53:45.850 --> 00:53:48.340
that we're getting
into a moral--

00:53:48.340 --> 00:53:50.030
human nature is human nature.

00:53:50.030 --> 00:53:54.990
And so these people who show
us and remind us of that--

00:53:54.990 --> 00:53:57.340
they're pretty scary people.

00:53:57.340 --> 00:54:00.010
I think when--

00:54:00.010 --> 00:54:01.360
I won't break my promise.

00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:04.075
When that person
starts with a T said--

00:54:04.075 --> 00:54:06.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:06.580 --> 00:54:08.740
--we don't want
these animals there,

00:54:08.740 --> 00:54:11.740
the people in the
neighborhood were relieved,

00:54:11.740 --> 00:54:14.290
but the people who had
private estates or gates

00:54:14.290 --> 00:54:16.380
were shocked, like
Nancy Pelosi's got

00:54:16.380 --> 00:54:18.650
a nice walled estate in Napa.

00:54:18.650 --> 00:54:21.700
So we've created, in our own
society, people that have--

00:54:21.700 --> 00:54:23.760
I don't want to
be to deprecatory,

00:54:23.760 --> 00:54:25.870
but they have pretensions
about human nature,

00:54:25.870 --> 00:54:28.000
but they're never subject
to the ramifications

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:28.917
of their own ideology.

00:54:28.917 --> 00:54:31.910
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:39.382 --> 00:54:41.090
AUDIENCE: I'm going
to tempt you to break

00:54:41.090 --> 00:54:46.850
your promise because I'm
sure you heard there was

00:54:46.850 --> 00:54:50.630
a recent report that Rex
Tillerson and General

00:54:50.630 --> 00:54:57.450
Mattis brought President Trump
to the Pentagon to brief him--

00:54:57.450 --> 00:55:04.010
to educate him-- on the
importance of America's

00:55:04.010 --> 00:55:05.810
alliances and so forth.

00:55:05.810 --> 00:55:09.090
And the report came
out that, at one point,

00:55:09.090 --> 00:55:14.180
Trump got so angry that
he yelled at the generals

00:55:14.180 --> 00:55:16.340
and said, you're
a bunch of losers,

00:55:16.340 --> 00:55:18.460
or something to that effect.

00:55:18.460 --> 00:55:22.320
And lo and behold,
after he got in,

00:55:22.320 --> 00:55:27.480
he changed the rules of
engagement, and we beat ISIS,

00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:28.120
and so forth.

00:55:28.120 --> 00:55:30.930
But just wondering if that's
another example of what

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:33.125
you're describing.

00:55:33.125 --> 00:55:35.750
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: We have an
oath at the Hoover Institution.

00:55:35.750 --> 00:55:37.208
We're not allowed
to speak publicly

00:55:37.208 --> 00:55:38.550
about another senior fellow.

00:55:38.550 --> 00:55:41.480
So I have great respect
for General Mattis,

00:55:41.480 --> 00:55:46.130
but in a larger
sense, I think what

00:55:46.130 --> 00:55:51.950
Trump was trying to say
is that he values optics--

00:55:51.950 --> 00:55:55.020
he's reality TV-- and ratings.

00:55:55.020 --> 00:55:56.900
Remember, every time
he gets mad at CNN,

00:55:56.900 --> 00:55:59.030
he doesn't just say
that CNN is fake news.

00:55:59.030 --> 00:56:00.290
He always said, low ratings.

00:56:00.290 --> 00:56:01.922
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:01.922 --> 00:56:03.380
Or he says, have
you seen that guy?

00:56:03.380 --> 00:56:05.690
He looks really bad on TV.

00:56:05.690 --> 00:56:08.780
So in his way of
thinking, he's practical.

00:56:08.780 --> 00:56:10.310
And when you look
at Afghanistan,

00:56:10.310 --> 00:56:12.650
it's bad ratings and bad optics.

00:56:12.650 --> 00:56:16.610
But he's also got a
pre-civilizational animal

00:56:16.610 --> 00:56:17.540
cunning.

00:56:17.540 --> 00:56:18.890
And why wouldn't you?

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:21.590
How could you succeed in
the Manhattan real estate

00:56:21.590 --> 00:56:25.970
market dealing with crooked
unions, crooked politicians,

00:56:25.970 --> 00:56:28.070
crooked community?

00:56:28.070 --> 00:56:29.870
You got the builders union.

00:56:29.870 --> 00:56:31.370
You got Al Sharpton.

00:56:31.370 --> 00:56:32.457
You've got Bill de Blasio.

00:56:32.457 --> 00:56:34.790
How do you deal with all of
them and build a skyscraper?

00:56:34.790 --> 00:56:38.960
So he has certain skill
sets, and those skill sets

00:56:38.960 --> 00:56:43.070
are not compatible
with administrative--

00:56:43.070 --> 00:56:45.350
whether it's civilian
or military-- parlance

00:56:45.350 --> 00:56:48.600
and the way you solve problems.

00:56:48.600 --> 00:56:51.740
And I guess the only way we
could reconcile that paradox

00:56:51.740 --> 00:56:55.040
is that you wouldn't want
a government of Trump's,

00:56:55.040 --> 00:56:56.850
or Patton's, or those
types of people,

00:56:56.850 --> 00:56:59.450
but you might want
one or two of them

00:56:59.450 --> 00:57:04.490
on occasion when the
system finds itself

00:57:04.490 --> 00:57:06.830
incapable of doing things.

00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:11.960
So if you haven't had worker's
wages increases in 12 years,

00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:17.390
or you haven't had 3.5%
unemployment in 50 years,

00:57:17.390 --> 00:57:20.010
or African-American
unemployment in seven years,

00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:23.750
then maybe half the
country decided,

00:57:23.750 --> 00:57:26.570
I'm willing to put up with the
President of the United States

00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:28.760
saying that Jerry
Nadler is a sleazebag.

00:57:28.760 --> 00:57:30.610
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:57:31.496 --> 00:57:39.380
And then just as a
[INAUDIBLE] to that,

00:57:39.380 --> 00:57:42.923
I was really struck when
I hear people that voted

00:57:42.923 --> 00:57:44.090
for him that talk about him.

00:57:44.090 --> 00:57:45.980
It reminds me so
much about reading,

00:57:45.980 --> 00:57:49.010
when I was researching
a book on Patton,

00:57:49.010 --> 00:57:50.810
news reports about Patton.

00:57:50.810 --> 00:57:55.640
Because usually you hear, well,
I really like what he did,

00:57:55.640 --> 00:57:57.330
I just wish he
wouldn't say that.

00:57:57.330 --> 00:57:59.030
I just wish he wouldn't tweet.

00:57:59.030 --> 00:58:01.220
And they would say
things about Patton.

00:58:01.220 --> 00:58:05.600
Wow, I really liked the
idea that he beat Montgomery

00:58:05.600 --> 00:58:08.297
to Messinia, but why'd
he have to do that?

00:58:08.297 --> 00:58:09.380
And same thing with LeMay.

00:58:09.380 --> 00:58:11.840
Why did he do that?

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:14.600
Curtis LeMay created the
Strategic Air Command.

00:58:14.600 --> 00:58:16.940
Essentially, he inherited
it, but it was nothing.

00:58:16.940 --> 00:58:20.210
And when he left it,
it had 200,000 people.

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:23.990
It was the most sophisticated
deadly air force in the world.

00:58:23.990 --> 00:58:27.410
And the reward for that
was being caricatured

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:31.070
by George C. Scott
or Buck Turgidson,

00:58:31.070 --> 00:58:35.630
both characters in Dr.
Strangelove, as a mad nut.

00:58:35.630 --> 00:58:37.660
And he was anything but a nut.

00:58:37.660 --> 00:58:40.220
He's a very sophisticated,
educated person.

00:58:40.220 --> 00:58:42.300
Omar Bradley couldn't
speak a word of French.

00:58:42.300 --> 00:58:43.450
You would never know it.

00:58:43.450 --> 00:58:46.990
And he had a very beautiful
cabin with upholstery

00:58:46.990 --> 00:58:48.920
that he towed out in
the battlefield that

00:58:48.920 --> 00:58:50.150
was his personal--

00:58:50.150 --> 00:58:52.588
Patton slept on the ground.

00:58:52.588 --> 00:58:54.380
Patton walked around
to make sure everybody

00:58:54.380 --> 00:58:56.930
had dry socks so they
wouldn't get trench foot,

00:58:56.930 --> 00:58:58.610
but it didn't fit the image.

00:58:58.610 --> 00:59:01.640
Bradley was a GI
general, but in fact,

00:59:01.640 --> 00:59:05.330
when people went on
leave into England

00:59:05.330 --> 00:59:07.340
and they were from the
1st Army, Hodge's Army,

00:59:07.340 --> 00:59:09.410
they would lie and
say they were 3rd Army

00:59:09.410 --> 00:59:10.340
because they thought
they would get

00:59:10.340 --> 00:59:11.548
better dates from the nurses.

00:59:11.548 --> 00:59:14.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:14.380 --> 00:59:15.700
I don't know if you have--

00:59:15.700 --> 00:59:18.263
SPEAKER 1: We have time
for one more question.

00:59:18.263 --> 00:59:19.930
AUDIENCE: First of
all, thank you again,

00:59:19.930 --> 00:59:22.950
Dr. Hanson, for speaking.

00:59:22.950 --> 00:59:26.350
You were talking about Patton's
strategies and approach

00:59:26.350 --> 00:59:27.850
to things, and how
different it was

00:59:27.850 --> 00:59:30.790
from pretty much every other
general of World War II.

00:59:30.790 --> 00:59:35.170
What would you say was
the reason for that?

00:59:35.170 --> 00:59:37.960
He was schooled at VMI, like
most of our other leaders were.

00:59:37.960 --> 00:59:40.803
But was there something like
his early days in the military,

00:59:40.803 --> 00:59:42.220
or do you think
that's just how he

00:59:42.220 --> 00:59:44.860
was as a person, that caused
him to approach things

00:59:44.860 --> 00:59:45.896
so differently?

00:59:51.292 --> 00:59:53.500
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Well,
his preeminent biographer,

00:59:53.500 --> 00:59:57.490
Carl de Estes, said he had a
number of serious concussions,

00:59:57.490 --> 00:59:58.305
traumatic brain--

00:59:58.305 --> 00:59:58.805
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:58.805 --> 01:00:00.640
He did.

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:02.290
He used it in the
context because there

01:00:02.290 --> 01:00:06.280
was a period in his life where
he did some despicable things,

01:00:06.280 --> 01:00:07.782
like date his step-niece.

01:00:07.782 --> 01:00:10.365
Or I shouldn't say date-- have
an affair while he was married.

01:00:10.365 --> 01:00:12.220
He had a wonderful wife.

01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:15.490
But there were certain elements.

01:00:15.490 --> 01:00:18.190
He did believe in reincarnation.

01:00:18.190 --> 01:00:21.040
And he did believe that
the Patton family--

01:00:21.040 --> 01:00:25.540
his grandfather was
killed in the Civil War,

01:00:25.540 --> 01:00:28.605
so he had this idea
that he had a destiny.

01:00:28.605 --> 01:00:30.340
And he kept saying,
I'm not going

01:00:30.340 --> 01:00:32.980
to be cheated out of my destiny.

01:00:32.980 --> 01:00:34.810
And he went into a
severe depression

01:00:34.810 --> 01:00:37.400
when he was not allowed
to fight in the Pacific.

01:00:37.400 --> 01:00:42.280
So he had this idea
that he was exceptional

01:00:42.280 --> 01:00:43.870
in his knowledge of war.

01:00:43.870 --> 01:00:46.210
And he would say
again and again,

01:00:46.210 --> 01:00:49.060
when they talked about
closing the Falaise Gap,

01:00:49.060 --> 01:00:51.280
or taking Palermo and
then making a right turn,

01:00:51.280 --> 01:00:56.092
or having amphib, he'd
always say to his staff,

01:00:56.092 --> 01:00:57.550
I don't know where
I got this idea.

01:00:57.550 --> 01:01:00.700
I woke up at 2:00 in the
morning with this idea,

01:01:00.700 --> 01:01:02.030
and I wrote it down.

01:01:02.030 --> 01:01:05.440
And then he would give
meticulous diagrams of which

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:08.290
particular battalions and
brigades he would move.

01:01:08.290 --> 01:01:09.940
And so he believed,
at least, he had

01:01:09.940 --> 01:01:14.210
some kind of artistic
inspiration about war.

01:01:14.210 --> 01:01:15.270
I think that's why--

01:01:15.270 --> 01:01:18.880
I think it was de Estes--
named Patton a genius for war.

01:01:18.880 --> 01:01:21.520
There was something
about him-- mystical.

01:01:21.520 --> 01:01:25.120
And the movie tried to bring
that out, maybe exaggerate it.

01:01:25.120 --> 01:01:28.300
But people around him
thought that he was not

01:01:28.300 --> 01:01:30.070
stable mentally.

01:01:30.070 --> 01:01:34.600
And he was completely unaware
of the effect, sometimes,

01:01:34.600 --> 01:01:38.050
he had on people who had
control over his destiny.

01:01:38.050 --> 01:01:41.110
So if he was on
maneuvers, and right

01:01:41.110 --> 01:01:43.420
in front of Eisenhower
and Bradley,

01:01:43.420 --> 01:01:46.960
he went over and screamed
at a lieutenant colonel,

01:01:46.960 --> 01:01:48.430
said you son of a bang.

01:01:48.430 --> 01:01:49.330
You did this.

01:01:49.330 --> 01:01:51.820
You're going to get men killed.

01:01:51.820 --> 01:01:54.670
And then he walked by
and smile, back thinking

01:01:54.670 --> 01:01:56.870
that Eisenhower was impressed.

01:01:56.870 --> 01:01:58.900
And Eisenhower later
would say, this guy

01:01:58.900 --> 01:02:01.300
is not fit for high command.

01:02:01.300 --> 01:02:05.980
So he was sui generis.

01:02:05.980 --> 01:02:08.410
We saw Sherman like
him, and I think

01:02:08.410 --> 01:02:12.490
there were elements like him,
as we heard today, in MacArthur.

01:02:12.490 --> 01:02:17.470
MacArthur had some
of the same traits,

01:02:17.470 --> 01:02:20.320
although he is much more
institutionalized than Patton,

01:02:20.320 --> 01:02:24.480
and much more politically savvy,
I think, at least sometimes.

01:02:24.480 --> 01:02:27.855
Is that it, then, or one more?

01:02:27.855 --> 01:02:29.230
SPEAKER 1: Thank
you, Dr. Hanson.

01:02:29.230 --> 01:02:30.522
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON: Thank you.

01:02:30.522 --> 01:02:33.150
[APPLAUSE]

